usb: gadget: configfs: fix use-after-free on gadget teardown

CONFIG_USB_CONFIGFS_UEVENT keeps a single global `android_device` that is
assigned by android_device_create() and never cleared. Three defects follow
from that, all reachable on this board because aiden-usb-gadget removes the
gadget directory and recreates it whenever the unit is restarted (an explicit
`systemctl restart`, or a systemd retry within StartLimitBurst). The ECM
watchdog is not a trigger: it only rewrites the UDC attribute and never drops
the configfs group.

  1. After the first gadget is destroyed, `android_device` still points at the
     device that device_destroy() freed. The next control transfer runs
     android_setup(), which does dev_get_drvdata(android_device) on that freed
     pointer. Derive the gadget_info from the gadget instead, which is what
     mainline does. The unsynchronised get_gadget_data() before the lock is
     only used to reach gadget_info; the second read inside gi->spinlock is
     kept because configfs_composite_unbind() clears the gadget data under
     that same lock, so it remains the race-safe check.

  2. gi->work (android_work) dereferences gi->dev->kobj. A work item still
     queued when the device is destroyed touches freed memory, so cancel it
     before device_destroy() and clear gi->dev afterwards.

  3. create_function_device() also parents new function devices to the stale
     `android_device`. Clearing the global on teardown turns that into a clean
     -EINVAL instead of a second use-after-free. Only f_midi and
     f_audio_source call it, and the Aiden composite instantiates neither, but
     CONFIG_USB_CONFIGFS_F_MIDI is enabled in the production kernel.

Assigning `android_device` after the attribute loop closes a smaller hole in
the same family: the previous order published the pointer before
device_create_file() could fail and destroy the device it points at.

diff --git a/sysdrv/source/kernel/drivers/usb/gadget/configfs.c b/sysdrv/source/kernel/drivers/usb/gadget/configfs.c
index 81f680852..6b00c9c2a 100644
--- a/sysdrv/source/kernel/drivers/usb/gadget/configfs.c
+++ b/sysdrv/source/kernel/drivers/usb/gadget/configfs.c
@@ -1549,10 +1549,11 @@ static int android_setup(struct usb_gadget *gadget,
 	int value = -EOPNOTSUPP;
 	struct usb_function_instance *fi;
 
-	if (!android_device)
+	cdev = get_gadget_data(gadget);
+	if (!cdev)
 		return 0;
 
-	gi = dev_get_drvdata(android_device);
+	gi = container_of(cdev, struct gadget_info, cdev);
 	spin_lock_irqsave(&gi->spinlock, flags);
 	cdev = get_gadget_data(gadget);
 	if (!cdev || gi->unbind) {
@@ -1788,8 +1789,6 @@ static int android_device_create(struct gadget_info *gi)
 		return PTR_ERR(gi->dev);
 
 	dev_set_drvdata(gi->dev, gi);
-	if (!android_device)
-		android_device = gi->dev;
 
 	attrs = android_usb_attributes;
 	while ((attr = *attrs++)) {
@@ -1802,6 +1801,8 @@ static int android_device_create(struct gadget_info *gi)
 			return err;
 		}
 	}
+	if (!android_device)
+		android_device = gi->dev;
 
 	return 0;
 }
@@ -1811,10 +1812,14 @@ static void android_device_destroy(struct gadget_info *gi)
 	struct device_attribute **attrs;
 	struct device_attribute *attr;
 
+	cancel_work_sync(&gi->work);
 	attrs = android_usb_attributes;
 	while ((attr = *attrs++))
 		device_remove_file(gi->dev, attr);
+	if (android_device == gi->dev)
+		android_device = NULL;
 	device_destroy(gi->dev->class, gi->dev->devt);
+	gi->dev = NULL;
 }
 #else
 static inline int android_device_create(struct gadget_info *gi)
